            "text": "<p><strong>Size</strong><br>Match the reel to your rod weight. Product pages list compatible line weights. A 5/6 reel sits on a 5- or 6-weight rod.</p><h3>Large arbor</h3><p>Faster retrieve, room for backing, fewer coils on the ground. Standard arbors still work on small water if you like the feel.</p><h3>Freshwater trout and bass</h3><p>Balance and reliability matter more than max drag. Click-and-pawl is fine on light rods if that is your style.</p><h3>Salt</h3><p>Stronger drag, corrosion resistance, and often a sealed drag. Rinse after salt. Oversizing a half step helps backing capacity.</p><h3>Brand aisles</h3><p>Use the chips for Echo, Redington, Ross, Sage, Lamson, and related shelves like <a href=\"/collections/hatch-fly-reels\">Hatch</a> and <a href=\"/collections/ross-fly-reels\">Ross</a>. Compare weight, arbor, and drag on each product page. Shop <a href=\"/collections/fly-rods\">fly rods</a> when you need the blank side of the pair.</p>"
